 test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.3$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.7$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519590 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4d56835-15e1-5dd9-b189-b250c69cae71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519590 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.3)| & =|1-0.3+\\frac{(0.3)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.1)| & =|1-1.1+\\frac{(1.1)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{101}{200}| \\\\  & =0.505<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.7)| & =|1-1.7+\\frac{(1.7)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{461}{200}| \\\\  & =2.305>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18bfeff9-da8e-5adf-b0b0-f3f134960c7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519622 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519622 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "236a5063-a320-5c79-a165-40f007e87d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519622 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186519590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50ccc479-0bdf-5248-b544-a38b78cc03a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519669 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f438fdd3-89dd-5c2a-9c44-3d2afb1adf2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519669 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186372684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519671 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519671 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd7e31ca-0961-5f9c-aed7-3bd1d98457f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519671 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308464 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
